Second Part
Form and Mechanism of Services for Violence Against Women and Children Article 21 (1) In the context of providing protection services to women and children from acts of violence, the Regional Government establishes the Regional Technical Implementation Unit for the Protection of Women and Children (UPTD PPA), Integrated Referral Service System (SLRT), Team for Violence Against Women/Children (Tim KTP/A) at community health centers, Hospitals, schools, Islamic Boarding Schools, Women-Friendly and Child-Caring Villages (DRPPA).
(2) The Regional Government through UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1), functions to implement services:
a. Public complaints;
b. Victim outreach;
c. Case management;
d. Temporary shelter;
e. Mediation;
f. Victim assistance; and
g. Victim repatriation.
(3) Further provisions regarding the organization and working procedures of UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation. (4) To assist in the protection of women and children, the Regional Government establishes:
a. Task Force for the Prevention and Handling of Criminal Acts of Human Trafficking;
b. Task Force for the Prevention of Child Marriage;
c. Task Force for Child-Friendly Regency;
d. Regional Action Committee for the elimination of the worst forms of child labor or Worst Forms of Child Labor (BPTA); e. Regional Children's Forum (FAD);
f. Community-Based Integrated Child Protection (PATBM) PENA BISMA;
g. Domestic Violence Forum (KDRT);
h. Task Unit for the Protection of Women and Children (Satgas PPA); i. Women-Friendly and Child-Caring Villages (DRPPA);
j. TIM KTP/A at Hospitals, Community Health Centers, schools, Islamic Boarding Schools.
(5) Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties, and functions of the task forces as referred to in paragraph (4) shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 22
(1) The form of Regional Government services to victims implemented by UPTD PPA and others as referred to in Article 21 includes:
a. Medical services;
b. Medicolegal services (visum);
c. Psychosocial services;
d. Legal services;
e. Economic independence services;
f. Psychological services; and
g. Psychiatric services.
(2) Further provisions regarding service mechanisms according to standard operational procedures are regulated in the Regent's Decree.

Article 27
Strengthening and development of Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions, is implemented through:
a. capacity building of Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions;
b. capacity building of human resources managing Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions;
c. assistance in implementing Women's Empowerment services;
d. strengthening networks among Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions at the district level
e. facilitation of efforts to fulfill standards of Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions; and
f. development of communication, information, and education regarding Women's Empowerment services

Article 31
(1) Funding for protection of women and children victims of violence includes:
a. Medical services, which include doctor examinations, treatment costs, hospital costs, medication costs and medical support costs;
b. Medicolegal services, which include examinations for Visum et Repertum and et Psikiatrikum;
c. Psychosocial services, which consist of counseling and psychological therapy as well as safe houses (shelters);
d. Legal services;
e. Economic empowerment, in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access.
(2) Funding management is carried out through technical institutions appointed by the Regional Government which is conducted transparently and can be legally accounted for.

